# {{infl of|en|spring||ed-form}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|volume=III|chapter=XII|page=245|passage=“When do you write to Colonel Brandon, ma’am?” was an enquiry which '''sprung''' from the impatience of her mind to have something going on.}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Fitted or cushioned with [[spring]]s.
#: {{nearsyn|en|spring-loaded}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''sprung''' mattress}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''sprung''' weight of a vehicle}}
# {{lb|en|of a spring or other springy object}} [[worn out|Worn out]] such that the [[springiness]] has dissipated.
#: {{ux|en|My old money clip was '''sprung''', so I replaced it.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|African American Vernacular English}} Utterly [[infatuated]] with someone; completely taken over by romantic interest; originally and literally, having a penile [[erection]].
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1992|composer=w:Sir Mix-a-Lot|title=w:Baby Got Back|album=w:Mack Daddy|passage=I like big butts and I cannot lie. / You other brothers can’t deny / that when a girl walks in / with a itty bitty waist / and a round thing in your face you get '''sprung'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2003|composer=w:Beyoncé; et al.|title=[[w:Crazy in Love (Beyoncé Knowles song)|Crazy In Love]]|album=w:Dangerously in Love|passage={{...}} / ’Cause your love got the best of me, / And baby, you’re making a fool of me. / You got me '''sprung''' and I don’t care who sees, / ’Cause baby, you got me so crazy.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2005|composer=w:Mariah Carey; et al.|title=Sprung|album=w:The Emancipation of Mimi|note=bonus track in some editions|passage=’Cause I’m '''sprung''' over you / And ain’t nothin’ I can do / {{...}} / Thoughts of you fill my head / {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2009|composer=w:Flo Rida; et al.|title=[[w:Sugar (Flo Rida song)|Sugar]]|album=w:R.O.O.T.S.|passage=My lips like sugar. / This candy got you '''sprung'''.}}
# {{lb|en|Australian|slang}} Caught doing something illegal or against the rules.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gabrielle Carey; Kathy Lette|title=Puberty Blues|year=1979|page=46|passage=`'''Sprung'''!' cried Jeff Basin, the local dubbo.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|dated}} [[drunk]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|nautical|of a [[spar]]}} [[crack]]ed or [[strain]]ed.

====Usage notes====
* The adjective {{m|en||sprung}}, unlike (say) {{m|en||infatuated}}, does not normally take a complement; a person may be infatuated ''with'' someone, but is simply ''sprung''. As with {{m|en|crazy}} or {{m|en|gaga}}, the target of the emotion is normally indicated by surrounding context; this is seen in the 1992 and 2003 quotations above. However, while relatively uncommon, it is possible for {{m|en||sprung}} to take a complement, construed with a preposition such as {{m|en|over}} (much like {{m|en||gaga}}); this is seen in the 2005 quotation above.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|infatuated}} {{l|en|smitten}}, {{l|en|taken}}; see also [[Thesaurus:in love]]
* {{sense|caught doing something illegal}} {{l|en|caught with a hand in the cookie jar}}, {{l|en|red-handed}}
* {{sense|cracked or strained}} {{l|en|crazed}}, {{l|en|splintered}}; see also [[Thesaurus:broken]]
* {{sense|drunk}} See [[Thesaurus:drunk]]

